1327b606 (2877 1794213463971 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . decision) (title . "A GROUP IS SENT TO ONLY WHILE ITS LEADER IS HELD") (src . "C2. A GROUP IS SENT TO ONLY WHILE ITS LEADER IS HELD: G = A's pid, and\n    a pgid cannot be taken by another process while A exists, reaped or\n    not; once A is reaped, a process that gets pid G and calls setsid or\n    setpgid leads a group G of its own (a daemon's detach does exactly\n    that), and `kill -G` would reach it. stopped_launch (:702) already\n    stops G before reaping A. The end path is reordered: the wait loop\n    (:721-726) detects A's end WITHOUT reaping it, the grace and\n    stop_group run with A a zombie holding G, and A is reaped after\n    (reap_anchor_bounded, D2, the function stopped_launch uses), its\n    status read from that reap. (v1.1) A'S END IS OBSERVED ON A PIPE, NOT\n    BY ps: W opens one more pipe before the fork (end_r, end_w, beside\n    go and note); A keeps end_w open for its whole life and never writes\n    to it; W closes its end_w after the fork. (v1.2, r2 finding 1) C\n    INHERITS end_w AT ITS FORK AND CLOSES IT AS ITS FIRST STATEMENT,\n    before the signal reset and the three opens (:558-563): the open of\n    `--out` blocks on a FIFO with no reader, and a stop to G reaches C\n    too, so a C held before exec would otherwise keep end_w open past\n    A's death and W would see no EOF until its deadline. After the\n    explicit close the holders are A and, for the instructions between\n    the fork's return and that close, C; nothing in that window blocks,\n    and a C stopped in it (stop_group stops G) keeps end_w only until\n    stop_group's KILL ends it, with the wait loop's deadline check still\n    in force. Close-on-exec ($^F is 2) remains the backstop at exec; no\n    descendant of C holds end_w; A does not exec. The write end's last\n    holder is therefore A once C has passed its close, and end_r reaches\n    EOF (sysread answers 0) at the instant A exits, zombie or not. The wait loop replaces its 50 ms\n    sleep by a select on end_r with the same 50 ms timeout, then reads;\n    EOF is \"A ended\"; the stop-request and deadline checks stay in the\n    loop as today. No ps call is added, so the launch-self rows that\n    shim ps (LS-12 empty, LS-13 hanging, NOTE-2 exit 3, CENSUS RETRY\n    hanging the first call) see exactly the ps calls they see today and\n    keep their readings; the brief lists them as controls. After the\n    grace and stop_group, reap_anchor_bounded reaps A: it is a zombie,\n    so the reap answers at once. (v1.2, r2 finding 5) A REAP THAT MISSES\n    IS SPECIFIED, NOT DELEGATED: reap_anchor_bounded (:651-660) answers\n    undef on a D2 timeout and on a waitpid error (reap_errno set), and\n    stopped_launch (:699-705) ignores that answer today, so there is no\n    existing 'unknown with its note' path to take. The reordered normal\n    path decodes the reap's answer as the wait loop decodes $? today\n    when it is defined; when it is undef the command's status cannot be\n    read: the result's status is 126 (the launcher's own failure code,\n    as for a fork that fails) and a note is written; (v1.3, r3 finding\n    3) THE REAP SUPPLIES THE STATUS AND NOTHING ELSE: the normal path's\n    cleanup and members are computed exactly as today (:741-753), the\n    grace's quiet_by first (an unreadable grace stays 'unknown' however\n    the stop went, members surviving the grace are 'left' even when the\n    stop then kills them), then stop_group (clean, survivor, unknown),\n    and that computation does not read the reap's answer; a reap miss\n    after a stop that answered clean leaves cleanup clean, after a grace\n    survivor leaves it left, after an unreadable grace unknown. The note\n    is \"anchor reap: no answer within D2\" for the\n    timeout and \"anchor reap: waitpid <errno text>\" for the error.\n    stopped_launch keeps its status (the stop's) and gains the same note\n    on a miss, nothing else. An undef is never decoded as exit 0.\n    write_result's contract (id, status, cleanup, member*, note*, end)\n    is unchanged. The branches are reached by a test-only hook read by\n    launch.pl, LAUNCH_TEST_REAP_MISS=timeout|error, documented in\n    launch.pl's header as a hook of the suite's own launcher: the first\n    makes reap_anchor_bounded return undef at once, the second return\n    undef with reap_errno ECHILD; unset, the function is unchanged. The\n    hook composes with the LS ps shims, so the rows below reach a reap\n    miss beside a grace survivor and beside an unreadable grace. The census excludes A's own line: a member\n    is a line with pgid G whose state does not begin with Z; the coder\n    reads census_run's parse (:300-) and reports whether the Z filter is\n    already there (r1 read it at :372) or added. The result's status,\n    notes and cleanup keep their meaning and order (write_result\n    unchanged); the anchor-gone path (:663-) is unchanged.") (rule . "C2") (brief . "f356c-verified-signal-sends") (rule-version . "v1.7") (verified-by "test/launch-self.sc") (parent . "mlmfhyau.278") (ord . 1))))
