31e3d6aa (2901 1794213463991 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . decision) (title . "THE RUNNER'S SENDS CHECK THE PROCESS IDENTITY INLINE, one definition") (src . "C3. THE RUNNER'S SENDS CHECK THE PROCESS IDENTITY INLINE, one\n    definition: `kill_if_same <pid> <identity> <sig>` in run-fixtures.sh,\n    the shape of retire-race's kill9_if_same (test/retire-race.sc:153):\n    sends only when both arguments are non-empty, `kill -0` finds the pid\n    and `bps -o lstart= -o command= -p <pid>` equals the recorded\n    identity, on ONE line with the send (SA-3's exemption is by line).\n    (v1.1) THE IDENTITY IS THE START INSTANT AND THE COMMAND LINE\n    TOGETHER: tracked_w's argv carries `--id $token.$launch_n` and the\n    result path under the run's snapshot directory, the probe child's\n    argv carries `$run_root/.probe.ss` (run_root holds the run token),\n    and probe_parent, a subshell of the runner, shows the runner's own\n    command line; a pid reused by a process whose argv repeats the run's\n    token is not a shape the suite can produce, so the instant settles\n    \"the same process\" and the command settles \"a process of this run\".\n    Each `&` whose pid the runner later signals records the identity at\n    once: tracked_w (:293), probe_parent (:634) and the probe subshell's\n    child (:628, inside the subshell, `child=$!; child_id=$(bps ...)`,\n    the trap installed first). The five sends become kill_if_same calls;\n    an identity that could not be read is printed once (\"<name>: start\n    identity not read, not signalled\") and the bounded waits run as\n    today. THE GUARANTEE, NARROWED, RULED BY THE MAIN SESSION UNDER THE\n    USER'S DELEGATION (r1 finding 1): the check closes the hazard the\n    ledger item names, a send to a pid the runner read seconds or\n    minutes earlier and the shell has since reaped. (v1.2, r2 finding 2,\n    stated accurately) IT DOES NOT CLOSE THE INTERVAL BETWEEN THE\n    COMPARISON AND THE kill BUILTIN: the send repeats no identity-bound\n    operation, so a process that exits, is reaped and has its pid taken\n    in that interval by ANY process (not only one of this run's shape)\n    receives the signal, and the interval is bounded by nothing but\n    scheduling (a descheduled runner lengthens it without limit). THE\n    FIRST RECORD IS VALIDATED, NOT TRUSTED: the identity read at `$!` is\n    kept only when its command field holds the expected marker, for\n    tracked_w `--id $tracked_id`, for the probe child `$run_root/\n    .probe.ss`, for probe_parent the runner's own command line as `ps -o\n    command= -p $$` prints it; a record without the marker is dropped\n    and printed once, \"<name>: start identity not read (pid <pid> shows\n    another command), not signalled\". For probe_parent the command\n    settles only \"a runner with this argv\", which repeats across runs\n    of the same command line; the instant settles the process, and the\n    brief says so. bash 3.2 offers no identity-bound send and the runner\n    has no owner process holding the children's identity, so the\n    residual is accepted and stated here and in the function's comment,\n    and SA-8 does not claim it closed. (v1.2, r2 finding 4, replacing v1.1's\n    trap) THE PROBE CHILD IS HANDED TO stop_probe BY ITS IDENTITY ON\n    DISK: the subshell writes its child's pid (as today, .probe-pid) and\n    its validated identity to `$run_root/.probe-id` (tmp and mv, read\n    whole or not at all) before its `wait`; its TERM trap does ONE thing,\n    a kill_if_same TERM with the identity it holds (an empty record: no\n    send), then exits without waiting, so the trap's budget is one bps\n    (5 s at most, milliseconds as a rule). stop_probe keeps TERM, a\n    bounded wait and KILL for the parent, with the parent's wait raised\n    from 5 to 7 s so a bps held to its alarm cannot outlast it; then it\n    finishes the child itself from the two files: kill_if_same TERM,\n    wait_gone 5, kill_if_same KILL, wait_gone 2. (v1.3, r3 finding 2)\n    stop_probe ANSWERS WHETHER IT OWNED THE END: 0 only when the parent\n    is gone and the child is seen gone (by its identity, or a .probe-pid\n    that alive() says is gone); 1 when the child could not be finished,\n    a missing or empty .probe-id (the subshell ended before its\n    publication, or the first read failed validation), an identity that\n    no longer matches, or a child still alive after the KILL's wait, each\n    printed once as \"probe child <pid or ?>: not owned at the end, left\n    to end by itself\". (v1.4, r4 finding 1) TWO FLAGS, BOTH CALLERS:\n    probe_done=1 says stop_probe ran to its end (the parent gone), as\n    today; probe_unowned=1 says the child was not owned at that end.\n    Both callers of stop_probe set them from its answer, the main path\n    (:658-662) and on_signal (:385). (v1.5, r5 finding 1) probe_unowned\n    is NOT an exclusive first branch, it is a verdict ADDED after the\n    evidence, and (v1.6, r6 finding 1) THE EVIDENCE PATH FOR AN UNOWNED\n    PROBE, AND FOR A RUN WITHOUT A BASELINE, IS THE TOKEN PATH (v1.7, r7\n    finding 1): count_leaks' first branch (:509) is taken when probe_done\n    is not 1, OR probe_unowned is 1, OR environments are not visible and\n    no baseline was captured (schemes_before empty: the unowned path\n    never captures one, :660-662, and on_signal's path, :385-386, never\n    reaches the capture), so each of those runs gets the token snapshot\n    and its LEAKED-PROCESSES pids and commands (:511-518) or its\n    failed-snapshot line; the env_visible branch (:526-548) is as today;\n    the by-name fallback (:552-566) is reached only with a baseline, so\n    no comparison against an unset baseline and no shell numeric\n    diagnostic can occur, and no flag combination falls through with\n    leaked left at its initial 0. Then the verdicts: when probe_unowned\n    is 1, \"LEAK COUNT NOT TAKEN: the probe child was not owned at its\n    end; whatever is still running was not looked at\"; when environments\n    are not visible and there is no baseline, \"LEAK COUNT NOT TAKEN: the\n    by-name count has no baseline (the run ended before it was taken);\n    whatever is still running was not looked at\"; each sets leaked=1\n    whatever the evidence concluded (an empty token snapshot cannot say\n    a hidden-environment process is gone). So a leaked process is still\n    named with its pid and command, and neither an unowned probe nor a\n    run interrupted before its baseline can read as clean. The no-send rule for an unknown\n    identity is kept; what changes is that its consequence is said, not\n    absorbed. Nobody waits on a child not seen gone; the parent is `wait`ed as\n    today (:326), the child is init's once the parent is gone and is\n    never `wait`ed. v1.1's inner wait_gone and inner KILL are gone with\n    it: the trap and stop_probe no longer race the same five seconds.\n    Zombies: `kill -0` finds a zombie and ps still prints its lstart\n    and command, so a TERM to one is sent and lost, as today.") (rule . "C3") (brief . "f356c-verified-signal-sends") (rule-version . "v1.7") (verified-by "test/runner-self.sc") (parent . "mlmfhyau.278") (ord . 2))))
