683f9bd7 (2922 1794213464019 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . decision) (title . "THE WORKER'S OWN SIGNALS GO THROUGH THE HANDLE") (src . "C4. THE WORKER'S OWN SIGNALS GO THROUGH THE HANDLE: facade-proc's rows on\n    library workers pass a sender that maps each worker pid back to its\n    ref (the caller holds the refs; an alist pid -> ref built from the\n    same proc-pid reads) and calls worker-kill! for STOP, TERM and CONT\n    on the worker. (v1.1, r1 finding 3) THE STOP'S ANSWER GATES THE\n    LISTING: a sender answers #t when the signal was sent to the process\n    it holds and #f otherwise (worker-kill! answers proc-kill!'s #t/#f:\n    sent only while the handle is alive and the child 'running, under\n    with-interrupts-disabled, and the exit callback invalidates the\n    record before anything else; signal! answers the same way, the coder\n    reads test/signal-send.ss and wraps it if it does not). end-and-wait!\n    reads each STOP's answer, and (v1.2, r2 finding 3) #t MEANS SENT,\n    NOT STOPPED: the kernel stops the target when it next runs, so\n    between the STOP's return and the stop the worker can still reap a\n    child, and #t says nothing about an exited worker whose exit\n    callback has not run. THE STOP IS CONFIRMED BEFORE THE LISTING: for\n    each worker whose STOP answered #t, end-and-wait! polls `ps -o\n    stat= -p <pid>` (the pid-state rule, bounded at 2 s) until the\n    state begins with T, AND THEN (v1.3, r3 finding 1) ASKS THE HANDLE:\n    `(send! pid 0)`, signal 0 through worker-kill! and proc-kill!, which\n    answers #t only while the handle is alive and the child 'running\n    (the exit callback invalidates the record before anything else), or\n    the library's own liveness read of the handle if proc-kill! does\n    not take 0 (the coder reads tcp.sc and says which). The order\n    settles ownership: the T was sampled at t1, the handle answered #t\n    at t2 > t1, so the handle's process had not exited by t2 and the\n    pid at t1 was that process, stopped; a bare T is never taken as\n    proof. A worker seen T and then #t is held (STOP cannot be caught,\n    the process is stopped and its pid stays held until CONT; a stopped\n    parent reaps nothing, so from the confirmation on no listed child's\n    pid can be released before its TERM), and only then are its\n    children listed; a worker not seen T within the bound (state Z, R,\n    S, or ps failing) is \"P-09: worker <pid> not held at STOP (state\n    <s>); its children cannot be listed\"; a worker seen T whose handle\n    then answers #f is \"P-09: worker <pid> exited during the stop's\n    confirmation; its children cannot be listed\"; either way not listed\n    and the bound waited out. EXCLUDED, STATED: a CONT, KILL or tracer\n    from outside between the confirmation and the TERM; no process of\n    the suite sends to a worker's pid but end-and-wait!, and no tracer\n    runs. A worker whose STOP answered #t is thus held only when\n    confirmed through the handle, and sent TERM\n    by pid through child-send! (library-send! renamed, its comment\n    rewritten) and CONT sent after; a worker whose STOP answered #f\n    (exited and reaped between the proc-pid read and the STOP, or never\n    held) is not listed: end-and-wait! prints \"P-09: worker <pid> not\n    held at STOP; its children cannot be listed\" and takes the path the\n    #f pid takes today (the whole bound waited out). A stopped parent\n    cannot reap, so a child that exits stays a zombie and its pid is\n    held until CONT, and every send to a child precedes the CONT. The\n    comment cites tcp.sc's guard by its rule, not its line.\n    end-and-wait!'s signature stays (maybe-pids send!); the order STOP,\n    list, TERM, CONT stays.") (rule . "C4") (brief . "f356c-verified-signal-sends") (rule-version . "v1.7") (verified-by "test/facade-proc.sc") (parent . "mlmfhyau.278") (ord . 3))))
